hi my name is david shaffer i'm a  professor of chemical and biomolecular  engineering bioengineering and molecular  install biology at uc berkeley  where i'm also director of the berkeley  stem cell center in addition i'm  co-founder of a company called 4d  molecular therapeutics that i'll be  talking about today  so the focus of the work that i'll be  discussing with you today is our efforts  over the past 20 years to  use a technique called directed  evolution to be able to create new  viruses  for therapeutic gene delivery so the  motivation for this work is that there's  serious unmet medical need within our  society  and depending upon which tissue you look  at you can typically see  two categories of diseases so taking the  nervous system in the upper left-hand  corner as an example  we have conditions that affect large  numbers of patients  where the genetics is not entirely clear  it's likely a combination of both  genetic as well as environmental  influences  such as alzheimer's or parkinson's  disease and we also have more rare  conditions  such as stories rhett syndrome  huntington's disease  amyotrophic lateral sclerosis which i'm  sure you otherwise known as lou gehrig's  disease  and these are conditions where you can  pin the blame on specific genetic  changes specific mutations  within particular genes of the human  genome  so regardless of which category of  condition you're looking at however  there's a number of situations in the  nervous system where people have  developed  treatments that can alleviate symptoms  for these horrible conditions  but there are no currently no cures that  are available so for all of these  conditions on this slide  it's quite clear that we need new new  treatments new therapies  so if we take a step back and consider  the basic biology that underlies all  biological function  uh we can see that of course uh dna  encodes  all the genetic information uh on the  planet and uh this information can then  be transcribed  within organisms into rna and then that  in turn becomes translated into a  protein and this  flow of information from dna to rna to  protein of course is called the central  dogma of biology  from the point of view of somebody who's  trying to develop a therapy we can view  each one of these levels as a potential  drug target  so the majority of pharmaceuticals that  are currently in use  target at the level of proteins small  molecules float around  typically bind hydrophobic pockets such  as the active site of an  enzyme and inhibit or alter the function  of that enzyme  monoclonal antibodies float around and  bind to protein targets floating on the  outside of the cell  such as a growth factor or receptor  if we move back one level you can begin  to develop therapies that work at  the level of rna inside of the cell so  these include rna interference or  anti-sense rnas whose goals are to  knock down the production of proteins  from rna or  messenger rna itself can actually be a  therapeutic molecule  but moving one back one further level at  the level of dna  this has really been the focus of my  entire career  so gene therapies the delivery of new  genetic information to a cell genome  editing the  changing or the editing of existing  genetic information inside of a cell  or cell therapies or stem cell therapies  where the goal is to deliver an entirely  new  so the power of targeting dna to be able  to effect a therapeutic outcome  is that the fact that dna unlike small  molecules or proteins  are actually  a tissue so if a new dna  to a cell or tissue that can potentially  persist for the life of a patient  which raises the possibility of single  administration long-term patient benefit  so just to illustrate this idea we have  here the  um on the x-axis the number of years  that the  individual is affected by a particular  condition so typically post-diagnosis  parkinson's patients  are on the order of another 16 years or  so  so congestive heart failure between  diagnosis ultimately when a patient  passes away  due to chf is on the order of 10 years  age-related  macular degeneration it itself is an  addition  it robs people of their vision but  typically does so  towards the life span so they typically  die of other causes after around 10  years of  diagnosis of amd so if we consider or  compare these to the lifetimes  the pharmacokinetic half-lifes of  therapeutic modalities that are  being developed to be able to treat  these conditions we have a small  molecule with a pharmacokinetic  half-life on the  on the order of hours monoclonal  antibodies last  days to several weeks however dna in the  form of a gene therapy  um can potentially last years or even  decades  so here are a couple of examples of gene  therapies one of which i'll be  discussing in more detail  where a single administration back in  the 2000s  led to uh in some cases over a decade of  therapeutic benefit from the patients  who were still enjoying the fact that  they received this gene therapy  many years ago so let's all get on the  same page and actually define what gene  therapy is  it's the delivery of genetic material to  the cells of an individual patient for  therapeutic benefit  and so while this idea was originally  proposed back in the 1960s  we've had to wait for 50 years in order  for it to begin to come to fruition  and the reason is this word highlighted  in yellow here delivery  which is the fact that dna from the  point of view of a cell or an  organ is a very large molecule it's  difficult to get it past the cell  membrane and into the nucleus of a cell  and in addition on its own floating  around in the bloodstream dna is not  particularly stable  it's degraded by nucleases that are  present  so it's been hard to be able to develop  delivery vehicles or vectors that can  carry dna inside of enough cells within  it to begin to move the meat the needle  on treating a disease  so the way that this has been achieved  increasingly o