led  retrograde transport  so specifically we know neurons have a  cell body where the nucleus is  and then they extend for the extent  axons in some cases uh millimeters or  many centimeters inside the body  and we found that if you deposit an aav  right at the end of one of these axons  um the the axon can actually internalize  the virus and traffic it back  retrogradely along that axon for very  long distances in some  cases infect  remotely if you will and this was  utilized subsequently by brian kaspar in  a study back in 2003  in which he injected aav into the muscle  of a mouse  and the virus was internalized by the  axons of motor neurons  and the motor neurons then retrogradely  trafficked the aav back to the spinal  cord  and this is a way of being able to  mediate high efficiency or relatively  high efficiency delivery to the spinal  cord of that mouse  after a simple intramuscular injection  leaking the virus brain barrier and use  this to  to mediate a protective factor into the  uh central nervous system a mouse model  of amitropic lateral sclerosis and this  extended animal life ban  so a company began to develop this  retrograde transport technology and  unfortunately found out that the  efficiency of retrograde transport  was too low it worked reasonably well  within a mouse less well within a rat  and not well at all within a non-human  primate and so it became  too easy to scale up for human clinical  use even though this could be a  very effective approach to be able to  get spread within the central nervous  system  as i'll show you in a couple of examples  so we began to to conduct uh av  evolution for retrograde transport and  the system that we utilized is uh shown  right here  um this was initially done within a  mouse and the  the cortex of these animals uh sends  projections  over a long distance to a centralized  region called the ponds  so the idea is that you could do an  injection of the library inside  the pons and some cortical neurons would  would end up internalizing that virus  and retrogradely transporting it back to  the cortex  and as a result with a single focal  injection you could get the virus to  spread through a large volume within the  cortex  of the brain so we repeated this  evolution multiple times  and ended up with one dominant variant  emerging we then loaded it  with the transgene that would end up  resulting in expression of a td tomato  and you can see a single injection into  the ponds result in a very widespread  expression across the brain  so we wanted to to quantify the level of  uh  increase in this retrograde transport so  we ended up  loading several different aavs  with the cargo that resulted in  expression of gfp just within the  nucleus  this is td tomato across the cytosol of  cells so it's kind of hard to count  individual neurons  this was a nuclearly localized cargo so  we can see that  upon injection into the pawns in the  lower left hand side  you should get expression across the  cortex in the upper right hand side  there's none  in the case of ab2 in the case of this  particular variant called a  dj this this came out of stanford you  can see at least in the bottom panel  that there's some neurons um  in the upper right hand side and the  cortex expressing gfp  but it's not comparable it's this  variant the av2 retro that we created  we can see widespread transduction  across those neurons  when we quantify the efficiency of  delivery you can see that  compared to av-1 to 8-9 we have  a 50-100 fold higher levels of  retrograde transport  and it compares quite well to a number  of other approaches for retrograde  transport  including even this fluorescent dye in  an organic molecule called fluoro gold  which has been utilized for  retrograde transport labeling  we then wanted to see strategically with  a couple of different injections how we  could maximize spread across the brain  and you can see here if we do three  different injections with three  different aavs of three different colors  um red green and blue into the superior  colliculus  thalamus and ponds respectively um just  these three focal injections gets you  wider spread expression across  large volumes inside the brain and we've  since utilized this to  affect genome editing within the brain  as described in that article in the  bottom right hand side  so i'd like to to move on quickly to the  the chapter the work i wanted to  describe to you today which is  um going to be some work that carries on  the same concept of solving problems  through libraries and evolution  but now applies it towards a different  problem which is av production  so there's been as gene therapy has been  increasingly successful  there's been a a bottleneck within the  field related to av manufacturing as  shown here in this relatively old  article from the uh the new york times  and this problem is illustrated here uh  so  av serotype nine which is the serotype  that's used for that  fda approved product to be able to treat  spinal muscular atrophy  so from around a 500 meter square  bioreactor you can create on the order  of 10 to the 16th viral particles  so you can then translate in this into  how much surface area you need to be  able to treat individual patients  who are suffering from certain  conditions so in the bot in the middle  here we have one sma patient  and they typically treat uh this is  novartis um avexis  they treat uh patients um typically on  the order of weeks of life so they're  treating young young patients just  post-natally and as a result you don't  need as much virus because simply  you know young patients aren't as big  yet so even so you need around 50 square  meters to do a patient  and av9 is also in clinical trials to be  able to treat duchene muscular dystrophy  and in that case you need 500 meters  squared and otherwise in other words  in some cases a single manufacturing run  per dmd patient treated  so how does this translate to the  incident and prevalent populations  you need around 20 000 meters square to  treat the annual incidence of a spinal  muscular atrophy type one  and you need around seven and a half  million square meters to be able to  treat the prevalent uh  duchenne muscular dystrophy population  so this is is actually hundreds of  around the order of a thousand  uh soccer ball fields uh worth of  surface area  so they're quite simply it's going to be  challenging to have enough manufacturing  capacity to to treat  these conditions so one way in which you  can ease the manufacturing burden is by  creating an aav that's much more  targeted and efficient at being able to  reach a tissue  so let's say you created a directed  evolution aav that's 100 fold better  getting there well that's going to  alleviate the manufacturing burden  but another way in which you could do it  is to actually engineer the  manufacturing cells to pump out more av  and we begin  to do that using a based upon  investigators within the field of n wide  expression perturbations where um in  some cases they're actually using a  version of cas9 fused to uh  transcriptional activator system and  then as a result  guide rnas will guide this  transcriptional activator this  effectively a synthetic transcription  factor to  you know depending upon the guide many  different locations of the genome and so  you can develop genome-wide activation  libraries or you could use a cas9  that has an active nuclease and got  using a guide rna  target that cas9 towards let's say the  first exon of every gene within the  human genome  and generate genome-wide knockout  libraries and so these  guide rna libraries are what direct  these different  systems either a transcriptional  activator or a knockout system  towards particular regions within the  genome  so we ended up embedding these guide  libraries  into aav vectors so that we could select  for  guides or specifically for genes whose  over expression or whose knockout  could increase aav production and we did  this within 293 cells  which is the dominant manufacture cell  line to produce produce aavs  so the rationale for this was that just  like we'd anticipate that  nature is never evolutionarily rewarded  in an aav  capsid for the reach of photoreceptor  neuron inside deep inside the eye  you know just it's not part of the aav  life cycle um  likely nature has never evolutionarily  rewarded a 293 cell which is a  a transformed cell line that was derived  from embryonic kidney  human embryonic kidney is never  evolutionarily rewarded for  for the ability to pump out aav so the  opportunity to engineer  the cell line to be able to better do  that  so the way that we did this is that we  created libraries of guide rnas inside  of av where we cloned in  all seventy thousand guides present  within this initial system  which contains uh the seventy thousand  contains three guides per gene  for each of uh 23 000 genes within the  human genome  or the gecko the knockout library  contains three guides for each of  nineteen thousand genes so  fifty eight thousand guide library so we  cloned those inside of aav  and packaged it along with the wrap cap  helper and an adenoviral helper  so that we created libraries of aav  vectors where every single variant on  the right hand side  can has the same capsule gene in this  case but contains a different guide rna  inside of it  we can then take these and infect it  infect cells with them at a low ratio of  virus to cell so each cell took up  only one virus and as a result expresses  only one guide rna  and these cells now have the crispr cast  system  which can take the guides and either  over express or  knock out a particular gene uh depending  upon the nature of the av library  so we then gave this the cell around 48  hours to perturb gene expression through  that over expression or that knockout  and uh then uh transfected in the  helpers  so that we could then use that cell to  manufacture aav  and the logic is that if a particular  over expression  of a given gene in the human genome or a  knockout of a given gene in the human  genome  ended up changing the cell in such a way  that it could better produce  aav then that cell is going to pump out  more aav  and just to remind you that aav genome  is  getting inside of it the barcode the  information that tells us  which gene perturbation resulted in the  better production of aav  so we can then harvest that library use  it to reinfect the cells  repackage and as time goes on the av  variants that or the guide rnas if you  will inside those aavs that results in  better packaging  are going to get exponentially amplified  within the population  and at the end we can then do um  sequencing  uh next-gen illumina sequencing to  sequence the guides and tell us  which genes uh whose perturbation would  result in an  increase in aav production so we have a  number of different hits  and two particularly interesting hits  emerged from the over expression library  uh one is called itp rip which is an  asymptote triphosphate receptor  interacting protein  so this modulates calcium release inside  of cells in response to  signals that release that result in itp  production  and scotu spindle and kinetochore  associated protein type 2 which is  involved so we took  cells 293 cells and over expressed these  two proteins  initially by over expressing the guide  and cas9 system  and then we did it dna encoding these  proteins and over expressing them inside  of cells  and we found out that upon over  expression of these proteins we get  around  a two to three fold increase behavior  production from these cells  and uh this is both at the level of  vector genomes produced  as well as infectious units produced and  furthermore  there was the possibility that these two  genes could be acting independently  within those cells and therefore by  combining them we can get synergistic  increases  and we see that upon over expressing  both cells we get around a four to five  fold increase  in in vector production and furthermore  this was not limited to aav2 but  extended to  other av stereotypes such as ab6 which  is notorious for being a particularly  difficult beat to produce and we see a  two to four around a three to four  increase in both viral tighter  genome tighter as well as infectious  unit titer  so we have since this investigating  mechanisms by which over-expression of  these genes  ends up resulting in better aav  production and we're seeing evidence  that these genes actually  perturb cell cycle in a way that is  advantageous for aav  production and we look forward to going  into detail into that mechanism soon  but i'd like to close now with a few  bullet points from the work that i have  presented the first is that gene therapy  is working  in addition recombinant safe viruses  like aav can be can represent very  potent therapeutic pharmaceutical gene  delivery vehicles  but of course nature didn't optimize  them for uses as use  in human therapies and therefore it's up  to re up to us to re-optimize these  viruses  to be safe effective efficient uh  therapies  so these particles are very complex  products of millions of years of  evolution and it's very difficult to  rationally engineer  changes into them however directed  evolution ends up being a very powerful  search algorithm  where we can search billions of  potential viral sequences to be able to  isolate the variants that have highly  optimized properties  for delivery in principle to any cell or  tissue target through  an optimal route of administration and  we use this approach to create avs that  have a highly  um that are highly efficient can target  delivery or in work that i didn't talk  about today  can also get past that pre-existing  antibody problem within the human  population  so one of the av very we've created has  already been in the clinic for almost a  year and a half and  uh several additional variants are going  to be entering into the clinic this year  so i'd like to acknowledge the people  who actually did the work uh the work  within the retina  was the product uh this is mouse retina  the product of leah burn denise dalcara  and tim day  in collaboration with john flannery at  uc berkeley uh the work  in the dog retina uh was the product of  of  again leah byrne at uc berkeley and john  flannery  in collaboration with welling beltran  university of pennsylvania  the work in the non-human primate retina  uh came out of  4d molecular therapeutics and i  especially like to call out david curran  my co-founder  and her ceo and  the work on retrograde transport was a  product of tom guy and bombio wong on  collaboration with investigators  at genealia farms and uh finally uh the  work  on the uh development of better av  manufacturing cell lines uh was the  product of chris barnes and david ojala  so thank you so much and uh thank you  for your time  and listening to our work
 

